# DNA-AND content · small bodies: asteroids, comets, dwarf planets, belts and clouds, meteors
# researched: Cambridge "Asteroid compositional types and their distributions"; Tholen classification;
#   Ohio State asteroid and trans-Neptunian lectures; Jewitt 2002 (comet nuclei); Encyclopedia.com
#   "Vital Statistics of Asteroids and Comets"; Highline College comets and asteroids notes
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r05-universe.md

# ── Asteroids by makeup ──

@asteroid c-type
name: C-type asteroid
real: yes
says: very dark, carbon-rich and primitive, little changed since the system formed; the most common type
linked-meteorite: carbonaceous chondrite

@asteroid s-type
name: S-type asteroid
real: yes
says: stony and silicate, brighter; more common close to the star
linked-meteorite: ordinary chondrite, stony-iron

@asteroid m-type
name: M-type asteroid
real: yes
says: nickel and iron, probably the broken metal core of a shattered small world
linked-meteorite: iron
example: Psyche

@asteroid e-type
name: E-type asteroid
real: yes
says: very bright and reflective

@asteroid p-type
name: P-type asteroid
real: yes
says: very dark, reddish, comet-like, found far out

@asteroid rubble-pile
name: Rubble-pile asteroid
real: yes
says: a loose heap of rocks held together only by its own weak gravity

@asteroid binary-asteroid
name: Binary asteroid
real: yes
says: two asteroids orbiting each other, or an asteroid with its own tiny moon

# ── Asteroids by orbit ──

@asteroid main-belt
name: Main-belt asteroid
real: yes
says: in the asteroid belt between the rocky planets and the giants

@asteroid near-earth
name: Near-planet asteroid
real: yes
says: an orbit that crosses close to a planet's; the ones that can hit

@asteroid trojan
name: Trojan asteroid
real: yes
says: sharing a planet's orbit, 60° ahead or behind it

@asteroid centaur
name: Centaur
real: yes
says: half asteroid, half comet, orbiting between the giant planets

# ── Comets ──

@comet short-period
name: Short-period comet
real: yes
from: Kuiper belt and scattered disk
says: returns every few years to a few hundred years

@comet long-period
name: Long-period comet
real: yes
from: Oort cloud
says: falls in once in thousands of years or more, sometimes only once

@comet sungrazer
name: Star-grazing comet
real: yes
says: swoops so close to its star that it often breaks up or burns away

@comet interstellar
name: Interstellar comet
real: yes
says: a comet from another star passing through once

@part comet-nucleus
name: Nucleus
real: yes
says: a dark ball of ice and dust a few kilometers across

@part comet-coma
name: Coma
real: yes
says: a cloud of gas and dust that boils off as the comet nears its star, up to about 100,000 km wide

@part comet-hydrogen-cloud
name: Hydrogen cloud
real: yes
says: a huge thin cloud of hydrogen around the comet

@part comet-ion-tail
name: Ion tail
real: yes
says: a straight, thin, glowing tail of gas pushed directly away from the star by the star's wind

@part comet-dust-tail
name: Dust tail
real: yes
says: a wider, curved tail of dust pushed out by starlight, bending along the comet's path

# ── Dwarf planets and far bodies ──

@dwarf ceres
name: Ceres
real: yes
where: the asteroid belt
note: the first asteroid ever found (1801), now a dwarf planet

@dwarf pluto
name: Pluto
real: yes
where: the Kuiper belt
note: a plutino, locked in a 3:2 beat with Neptune

@dwarf eris
name: Eris
real: yes
where: the scattered disk

@dwarf haumea
name: Haumea
real: yes
where: the Kuiper belt
note: spins so fast it's stretched into an egg shape

@dwarf makemake
name: Makemake
real: yes
where: the Kuiper belt

@far classical-kbo
name: Classical Kuiper-belt object
real: yes
says: icy body on a calm, round orbit in the Kuiper belt

@far plutino
name: Plutino
real: yes
says: icy body locked in a 3:2 orbital beat with the outermost giant, like Pluto

@far scattered-disk-object
name: Scattered-disk object
real: yes
says: icy body thrown onto a long, tilted orbit by a giant planet

@far detached-object
name: Detached object
real: yes
says: icy body on a huge lonely orbit beyond everything else
example: Sedna

@far oort-cloud-comet
name: Oort-cloud comet
real: yes
says: one of a vast number of icy bodies in the outer shell, tens of thousands of au out

# ── Meteors and meteorites ──

@meteor meteoroid
name: Meteoroid
real: yes
says: a small rock or grain drifting in space

@meteor meteor
name: Meteor
real: yes
says: the streak of light when a meteoroid burns up in a planet's air

@meteor fireball
name: Fireball
real: yes
says: a very bright meteor, sometimes exploding in the sky

@meteor meteor-shower
name: Meteor shower
real: yes
says: many meteors at once when a planet crosses a comet's dust trail

@meteor meteorite
name: Meteorite
real: yes
says: a rock that survives the fall and reaches the ground

@meteorite carbonaceous-chondrite
name: Carbonaceous chondrite
real: yes
says: dark, carbon-rich and full of the chemistry of life; from C-type asteroids

@meteorite ordinary-chondrite
name: Ordinary chondrite
real: yes
says: the most common stony meteorite, with tiny melted beads inside

@meteorite stony-iron
name: Stony-iron meteorite
real: yes
says: a mix of rock and metal

@meteorite iron
name: Iron meteorite
real: yes
says: nickel-iron, from the core of a shattered small world

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule small-seed
real: rule
says: every asteroid, comet and dwarf planet is generated live from the world seed; nothing is pre-built

@rule small-makeup-by-distance
real: rule
says: stony asteroids are more common close to the star, dark carbon-rich ones farther out, icy bodies beyond the frost line

@rule small-comet-source
real: rule
says: short-period comets come from the Kuiper belt and scattered disk; long-period comets fall in from the Oort cloud

@rule small-comet-tails
real: rule
says: comets grow a coma and two tails only near their star; the ion tail always points away from the star

@rule small-impacts
real: rule
says: near-planet asteroids and falling comets can hit worlds; impact size and effect follow the 10 impact sizes on the space page

@rule small-meteorites
real: rule
says: a meteorite's type follows the asteroid it came from; carbon-rich ones can deliver the chemistry of life

@rule small-resources
real: rule
says: metal asteroids are mined for metals, icy bodies and comets for water and fuel, carbon-rich asteroids for life chemistry
